**Example 17.** Calculate \(I\).10(3, 20).

Values of _IA_(_a_, _b_) for small integral \(a\) or \(b\) can conveniently be calculated using 26.5.6 or 26.5.7. Using 26.5.6 we have

\[1 - I_{.90}(20,3) = \frac{(.9)^{20}}{B(3,20)}\left\{ {\sum\limits_{i = 0}^{2}{(-1)^{i}{2\choose i}\frac{9!}{20 + i}}} \right\}\]

\[= \frac{.121576}{.216450 \times 10^{-3}}\left( .11030 \times 10^{- 3} \right) = .620040\]

**Binomial Distribution**

**Example 18.** Find the value of \(p\) which satisfies

\[\sum\limits_{s = 0}^{20}{50\choose s}p'q^{s - t} = .95,\quad\quad q = 1 - p\]

using 26.5.24 and Table 26.9.

Combining 26.5.24 and 26.5.27 we have

\[\sum\limits_{s = a}^{n}{n\choose s}p'q^{s - t} = Q(F|r_{1},r_{2})\]

where

\[r_{1} = 2(n - a + 1),r_{2} = 2(a),\text{ and }p = \frac{a}{a + (n - a + 1)F}\]

Hence

\[\sum\limits_{s = 0}^{20}{50\choose s}p'q^{s - t} = 1 - \sum\limits_{s = 1}^{80}{50\choose s}p'q^{s 0 - t} = 1 - Q(F|60,42) = .95\]

Harmonic interpolation on \(y\)2 in the table for which _Q_(_F_|_r_1, \(r\)2) = .05 results in _F_=1.624 for \(r\)1=60, \(r\)2=42, and thus _p_=\(\frac{42}{42 + 60(1.624)} = .301\). The correct answer to 4D is _p_=.3003.

**Approximating the Incomplete Beta Function**

**Example 19.** Find \(I\).60(16, 10.5) using 26.5.21.

Values of _IA_(_a_, _b_) can conveniently be calculated with good accuracy using the approximation given by 26.5.20 or 26.5.21. For this example (_a_+_b_-1)(1-_x_) = 10.20 which is greater than .8 and hence 26.5.21 will be used. Thus

\[w_{1} = \left[ (10.5)(.60) \right]^{1/\alpha} = 1.8469,w_{2} = \left[ 16(4) \right]^{1/\alpha} = 1.8566\]

\[y = \frac{3(1.8469)(.98942)-(1.8566)(.99306))}{\left[ \frac{(1.8469)^{2}}{10.5}\frac{(1.8566)^{2}}{16}\right]} = - .0668\]

and interpolating in Table 26.1 gives

\[P(-.0668) = 1 - P(.0668) = .47336\]

The answer correct to 5D is \(I\).60(16, 10.5) = .47332.

